<%= page_nav :about, "About · HTMX on Rails" %>

<section class="page-head">
  <h1>About this demo</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A Rails <%= Rails.version %> app whose entire client side is
    <%= link_to "htmx", "https://htmx.org", target: "_blank", rel: "noreferrer" %>
    — no build step, no framework — plus the
    <%= link_to "htmx gem", "https://alchemists.io/projects/htmx", target: "_blank", rel: "noreferrer" %>
    (<code>htmx <%= htmx_gem_version %></code>) for the Ruby side.
  </p>
</section>

<div class="grid grid-2">
  <section class="card">
    <header class="card-head">
      <h2>The gem in three calls</h2>
      <span class="tag">HTMX</span>
    </header>
    <p>
      <code>HTMX[...]</code> builds attributes: snake_case keys, dashed and prefixed
      on the way out. htmx accepts both <code>hx-</code> and <code>data-hx-</code>;
      the gem defaults to <code>data-hx-</code>, which is valid HTML for any element.
    </p>
    <%= code_sample :nav %>

    <p>
      <code>HTMX.request</code> parses the <code>HX-*</code> request headers into a
      Data object with <code>request?</code>, <code>boosted?</code> and
      <code>history_restore_request?</code> predicates. This app uses
      <code>request?</code> to decide between a full page and a fragment:
    </p>
    <%= code_sample :request %>

    <p>
      <code>HTMX.response!</code> writes the other direction. It maps the gem's names
      onto the <code>HX-*</code> response headers:
    </p>
    <%= code_sample :response %>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <header class="card-head">
      <h2>How the frame works</h2>
      <span class="tag">routing</span>
    </header>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>One shell.</strong> The layout renders the brand, the tabs and the
        footer once, around <code>&lt;main id="main-content"&gt;</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tabs are htmx links.</strong> Each one asks for a URL with
        <code>hx-get</code> and targets <code>#main-content</code>;
        <code>hx-push-url</code> keeps the address bar honest, so every tab is also
        a real, bookmarkable Rails route.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The layout is skipped for fragments.</strong> The controller returns
        the same template with <code>layout false</code>, so a tab click transfers
        a few kilobytes instead of the whole document.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The shell still updates.</strong> Because the nav lives outside the
        swap target, the fragment ships it back with
        <code>hx-swap-oob</code> — a second, smaller swap that moves the active tab
        and rewrites the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <%= code_sample :oob %>
  </section>
</div>

<section class="card">
  <header class="card-head">
    <h2>Stack</h2>
  </header>
  <table class="table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Rails</th><td><%= Rails.version %> (Propshaft, importmap, SQLite)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>htmx (browser)</th><td>2.0.10, vendored in <code>vendor/javascript/htmx.esm.js</code> and imported by <code>application.js</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>htmx (Ruby)</th><td><code>htmx <%= htmx_gem_version %></code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Hotwire</th><td>Not installed — Turbo Drive and Stimulus would compete with htmx for the same clicks, forms and DOM</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Database</th><td>None needed; the contact form is an <code>ActiveModel</code> object</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="muted">
    Tested at the HTTP layer: <code>test/controllers/pages_controller_test.rb</code>
    asserts the shell, the fragments, the out-of-band swaps and both form outcomes.
  </p>
</section>
